      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/0119821efe6d06a4d43278f09ab7ba4b471a59ffca94d1fe5d3bf67fd71c44a0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(12,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(12,4),$ we can substitute $x=12$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(12) \\\\ 16 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{3}$ is positive.",
