      "knowledge_point_id": "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/a16f3f2ba78b5aef180c4331d1dd99f37340d2c6d842c1596e35445f1dbf34c5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,0),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,6),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive.",
