      "problem": "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-1)$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-1),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(2-1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(1)(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
